Windows on the Galaxy S9+ (Exynos 9810)
=======================================

This project would not exist without the following work. It reuses code, and
follows designs, from the projects below; their authors are credited here and
the license texts this requires are reproduced at the end of this file. Our
thanks to them and their communities.

TianoCore / EDK2
  The UEFI firmware is built on EDK2 (https://github.com/tianocore/edk2),
  BSD-2-Clause-Patent. The Platform and Silicon packages here were written for
  this device, with the parts credited below adapted from other ports.

Project Silicium — Mu-Silicium (https://github.com/Project-Silicium/Mu-Silicium)
  Robotix22, N1kroks, halal-beef, Arminas Keršys and the Project Silicium
  contributors. BSD-2-Clause; individual files carry their own licenses.
  - Windows boot: the CNTFRQ_EL0 workaround Windows needs to boot on this SoC.
    firmware/Platform/Star2LtePkg/Library/PlatformBootManagerLib/MuCntfrqKep.c
    carries Mu-Silicium's Samsung CNTFRQ_EL0 shellcode unchanged
    (Silicon/Samsung/SamsungPkg/Library/ErrataPatchesLib/ShellCode.h at commit
    d5b3e9ebe74dc175c3c103c0966f8d245d38728e, Robotix22 and N1kroks) and the
    winload patch targets of its KernelErrataPatcherLib (Winload.h, Robotix22).
    MIT: Copyright (c) 2021 Samuel Tulach, Copyright (c) 2022-2023 DuoWoA
    authors, Copyright (c) 2026 Project Silicium (license text below).
  - Memory: firmware/Platform/Star2LtePkg/Drivers/Star2LteRamManagerDxe follows
    Mu-Silicium's RamManagerDxe (SiliciumPkg, Robotix22; Copyright (c) The Linux
    Foundation, Copyright (c) Sunflower2333): publishing the DRAM banks outside the
    PEI window from DXE with AddMemorySpace and the matching attributes. The
    memory-layout notes in Star2LtePkg.dsc compare against its RamManagerLib.
  - Its Exynos 9810 support (the exynos-refactor work) and its "Allow Windows
    Boot" path were the reference for how this firmware starts Windows
    (Star2LteAcpiPlatformDxe, PlatformBootManagerLib), and its Silicium-ACPI
    compiler is the iasl the build scripts use.

Renegade Project — edk2-msm (https://github.com/edk2-porting/edk2-msm), and
edk2-exynos (https://github.com/sonic011gamer/edk2-exynos)
  firmware/tools/bootshim/bootshim.S is adapted from tools/BootShim/BootShim.S
  of edk2-msm (Xilin Wu, Molly Sophia, Marijan Limov and the Renegade Project
  contributors), as carried by edk2-exynos, the Exynos fork of edk2-msm (J0SH1X
  and the edk2-msm contributors). The fake Linux kernel header and the
  relocating copy of the firmware to its load address are theirs; the interrupt
  masking, watchdog stop, cache disable and diagnostic breadcrumbs were added
  here. The bootshim is part of every published UEFI image. BSD-2-Clause
  (license text below). edk2-exynos was also the reference for this firmware's
  load address and firmware-volume size (Star2LtePkg.dsc and .fdf).

WOA-Project — Lumia950XLPkg (https://github.com/WOA-Project/Lumia950XLPkg) and
mu_andromeda_platforms (https://github.com/WOA-Project/mu_andromeda_platforms)
  Ben (Bingxing) Wang (imbushuo), Gustave Monce (gus33000), the DuoWoA authors
  and the WOA-Project contributors. BSD-2-Clause.
  - firmware/Platform/Star2LtePkg/Star2LtePkg.dsc and Star2LtePkg.fdf follow the
    structure of these PrePi-based Windows-on-ARM phone ports (a combined
    SEC+PEI that hands off to the DXE core, then BDS).
  - Patching the Windows boot path from UEFI before it runs (the
    KernelErrataPatcherLib approach behind MuCntfrqKep.c) comes from
    mu_andromeda_platforms, which built it on Samuel Tulach's rainbow
    (https://github.com/SamuelTulach/rainbow).
  - More broadly, WOA-Project's Windows-on-ARM phone work, which much of this
    project builds on.

The Linux kernel and the mainline Exynos 9810 / star2lte support
  Hardware bring-up knowledge (clocks, regulators, pinctrl, device tree) comes
  from the mainline Linux port of the Galaxy S9. GPL-2.0.

postmarketOS
  The postmarketOS community's star2lte work and documentation were an
  invaluable reference for booting a mainline stack on this device.

TheMorc (Richard Gráčik) — S6SY761Touch (https://github.com/TheMorc/S6SY761Touch)
  drivers/S6SY761Touch/ is derived from TheMorc's S6SY761Touch Windows driver
  (Richard Gráčik, with Gustave Monce and TAOcroatia), GPL-2.0, adapted here for
  the Galaxy S9+ (its HSI2C bus and touch-report handling). His driver is built
  on Microsoft's vhidmini2 KMDF sample (Windows-driver-samples, Copyright (c)
  Microsoft Corporation, Microsoft Public License, see
  drivers/Exynos9810Hsi2c/MS-PL.txt), began as a fork of Gustave Monce's
  nt36xxx_win (https://github.com/edk2-porting/nt36xxx_win, MIT), and ports the
  Linux sec_ts / s6sy761 driver by Andi Shyti and Samsung Electronics (GPL-2.0).
  The original copyright and license notices are retained in that subtree.

gahingwoo — wddm-mali-g52 (https://github.com/gahingwoo/wddm-mali-g52)
  drivers/MaliG76Probe/ is derived from its drivers/m1probe (commit 16f7e7c6),
  the KMDF driver that ran one Mali-G52 job under Windows on the RK3576,
  GPL-2.0, retargeted here to the Galaxy S10e's Mali-G76. The licence text is
  drivers/MaliG76Probe/LICENSE. Its register usage follows the Linux drm/panfrost
  driver (GPL-2.0).
  drivers/MaliG76Wddm/ is derived from its drivers/maliwddm (WDDM 1.3 driver),
  drivers/malidod (display-only driver), drivers/common/maligpu and
  include/maliwddm_abi.h, and its user-mode test tools/windows/m5test.c
  (commit 16f7e7c6),
  GPL-2.0, retargeted to the Galaxy S10e's Mali-G76 and internal panel. The
  licence text is drivers/MaliG76Wddm/LICENSE; maliwddm credits virtio-win's
  viogpu3d (BSD-3) for its shape (design only).

Microsoft Windows-driver-samples — SkeletonI2C
  The WDF/SpbCx framework scaffolding of drivers/Exynos9810Hsi2c/ comes from the
  spb/SkeletonI2C sample (https://github.com/microsoft/Windows-driver-samples).
  Those portions are Copyright (c) Microsoft Corporation and are used under the
  Microsoft Public License, included as drivers/Exynos9810Hsi2c/MS-PL.txt. The
  Exynos HSI2C engine itself is original work.

TWRP (Team Win Recovery Project)
  The recovery used to stage and write the Windows image on the device. TWRP is
  GPL-3.0 (https://github.com/TeamWin/android_bootable_recovery) and the kernel
  inside it is Samsung's GPL-2.0 Linux kernel. The installer re-skins the
  official twrp-3.7.0_9-0-star2lte.img from twrp.me on the user's PC into a
  Windows-Recovery-Environment look (tools/twrp-winre and
  installer/.../Twrp/WinReTwrpBuilder.cs). The releases also carry one such build,
  star2lte-winre-recovery.img, made from that official image with the open fonts
  below and the built-in gears. Every change is in this repository: the theme XML,
  the reskin rules (tools/twrp-winre/theme/reskin.xml), the /sbin helper scripts,
  the four-byte kernel patch that routes power-off (PowerOffRoutePatch.cs) and all
  artwork, which is original: every stock TWRP bitmap is replaced at build time by
  an original drawing of the same name and size (tools/twrp-winre/assets), so no
  TeamWin bitmap is committed or shipped in the built image. The four kernel
  modules baked into the recovery (rwd1_ack, rwd1_evidence_reader, rwd1_clear_poc,
  pram_smp_clear_poc) are our own GPL-2.0 code, with sources under
  tools/twrp-winre/modules/. The unmodified parts come from TeamWin's sources
  (https://github.com/TeamWin) and Samsung's SM-G965F kernel source
  (https://opensource.samsung.com); we will provide the corresponding source of
  the published recovery on request.

7-Zip LZMA SDK — Igor Pavlov
  installer/src/S9Woa.Installer.Core/Twrp/Lzma/ vendors the managed LZMA SDK,
  which its author placed in the public domain, so the installer can read and
  rewrite the recovery ramdisk (an "LZMA alone" stream) with no native
  dependency. Used verbatim except for the namespace and warning-suppression
  headers noted in that folder's README.

Segoe UI (Microsoft)
  The WinRE-look recovery the installer builds uses Segoe UI, which is copied from
  the *builder's own* %WINDIR%\Fonts at build time and written into that machine's
  recovery image. No Segoe font file is committed to this repository or
  redistributed with it; the recovery in the releases uses the open fonts below.

Selawik (Microsoft) — SIL Open Font License 1.1
  tools/twrp-winre/fonts/winre-*.ttf, the fonts of the published recovery, are
  compiled from Microsoft's open-source Selawik (https://github.com/microsoft/Selawik)
  by tools/twrp-winre/fonts/make_fonts.py. Copyright 2015 Microsoft Corporation, with
  Reserved Font Name Selawik; as Modified Versions they are renamed "S9WoA Sans".
  The license is tools/twrp-winre/fonts/OFL.txt and travels with the fonts.

UpdateOS gear animation (Microsoft)
  Optionally, the recovery shows Microsoft's UpdateOS rotating gears. The GIF is
  supplied by the user (for example in their build folder) and, like the fonts,
  is read only at build time on their own PC: its frames are rendered into that
  machine's recovery image and nowhere else. Neither the GIF nor any frame derived
  from it is committed to this repository or redistributed; without it, the
  recovery uses the original procedurally drawn gears in tools/twrp-winre.

Heimdall (https://github.com/Benjamin-Dobell/Heimdall, MIT) and
Thor (https://github.com/Samsung-Loki/Thor)
  The built-in Download-mode flasher implements Samsung's protocol as these
  projects document it. No code is copied from them.

tiny11builder (https://github.com/ntdevlabs/tiny11builder)
  The Lite profile follows the tiny11builder approach to trimming a Windows
  image; the Core profile follows its tiny11Coremaker.ps1 (package, Edge,
  OneDrive and WinRE removal, component store trim, registry defaults).

Microsoft
  Windows, the Windows App SDK and WinUI 3. Windows media is provided by the
  user or downloaded from Microsoft; no Windows files are redistributed here. The
  installer packages in the releases are self-contained: they include the .NET
  runtime (MIT) and the Windows App SDK runtime, redistributed under their own
  license terms, which ship with them in licenses\.

